<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- POGIL-style activity, Day 5 (~20-25 minutes). Begins right after finishing the last -->
<!-- two exercises of Activity 4 (zero-width and reverse-order) at the start of class. -->
<!-- First encounter with approximating a definite integral for a genuinely curved -->
<!-- function (not piecewise constant or piecewise linear), so none of the rectangle/ -->
<!-- triangle/trapezoid formulas from Activities 3-4 apply directly. Students discover the -->
<!-- rectangle-staircase idea themselves from a table + scatter plot (Exercise 1), then the -->
<!-- instructor names and formalizes the left/right Riemann sum together with the class -->
<!-- (Exercise 2), then a Desmos slider demo pushes n larger to motivate the limiting value -->
<!-- (Exercise 3). Deliberately AVOIDS summation notation and the general formula f(x_k): -->
<!-- that formalization is for the next activity. -->

<!-- Running example: f(x) = x^2 on [1,3], n=4, width 0.5, so every endpoint (1, 1.5, 2, -->
<!-- 2.5, 3) and every height (1, 2.25, 4, 6.25, 9) is a clean terminating decimal, and -->
<!-- none of the heights is zero (unlike starting the interval at x=0), so no rectangle is -->
<!-- degenerate. f is increasing throughout, so the over-/under-estimate reasoning is -->
<!-- unambiguous. True value: integral of x^2 from 1 to 3 = 26/3 = 8.6666..., which sits -->
<!-- between the left sum (6.75) and right sum (10.75) computed in Exercise 2. -->

<!-- BEFORE CLASS: open the linked Desmos graph yourself and confirm its function/interval/ -->
<!-- slider range still match this activity (f(x)=x^2 on [1,3]), or adjust it in Desmos to -->
<!-- match; the exact contents of that graph were not verified when this file was written. -->

<!-- Suggested pacing for a ~20-25 minute block (approximate, not enforced): -->
<!--   Intro ................................................................ ~2   min -->
<!--   Exercise 1: sketch your own approximating shape ..................... ~6   min -->
<!--   Exercise 2: left/right Riemann sums, talked through together ........ ~9   min -->
<!--   Exercise 3: Desmos slider, more rectangles, limit idea .............. ~6   min -->
<!--   TOTAL ...................................................................~23 min -->
<!-- -->
<!-- Where to cut if you're running short (in order of preference): -->
<!--   1. Exercise 3's task-check-n4 can be skipped (just jump straight to increasing n) -->
<!--      if the class already trusts the hand computation from Exercise 2. -->
<!--   2. Exercise 1's task-why-computable is a good check for understanding but can be -->
<!--      folded into the whole-class discussion instead of written individually. -->
<!--   3. Do NOT cut Exercise 2 (task-left-rule, task-right-rule, task-over-under) — this -->
<!--      is the entire point of the activity. -->
<!--   4. Do NOT cut task-limit-idea in Exercise 3 — it is the payoff that sets up the -->
<!--      next activity's formalization and, later, the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus. -->

<worksheet xml:id="activity-riemann-sum-approximation">
  <title>Approximating Area Under a Curve: An Introduction to Riemann Sums</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-riemann-sum-approximation">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Recognize that a curved function like <m>f(x)=x^2</m> is not built from flat or straight-line pieces, so the rectangle, triangle, and trapezoid formulas used so far don't directly give its area.</p></li>
      <li><p>From a table of function values, construct a region built only from horizontal and vertical segments (a staircase of rectangles) whose area can be computed exactly, as an approximation to the area under a curve.</p></li>
      <li><p>Construct, from the same table, both the left-edge and right-edge staircase approximations (the left and right Riemann sums), and compute their total areas.</p></li>
      <li><p>Determine, from the picture, which of the two approximations over-estimates and which under-estimates the true area, using the fact that the function is increasing.</p></li>
      <li><p>Use a dynamic, slider-based visualization to observe the left- and right-sums converging as the number of subintervals grows, anticipating the definite integral as a limiting value.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far, every rate function you've worked with has been piecewise constant or
      piecewise linear: built from flat pieces or straight-line pieces, so every region
      between the graph and the axis was a rectangle, a triangle, or a trapezoid, with an
      exact area formula ready to use. Today we start working with actual curved functions
      &#8212; functions like <m>y = x^2</m> &#8212; where no piece of the graph is a straight
      line, and none of those formulas apply directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      You'll build a way to approximate the area under a curve instead, using only tools you
      already have: rectangles. As always, the goal is to understand <em>why</em> the method
      works, not just to get a number.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-sketch-approximation" workspace="0in">
    <title>Sketching Your Own Approximation</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The table below shows the value of <m>f(x) = x^2</m> at five values of <m>x</m>,
        evenly spaced from <m>x=1</m> to <m>x=3</m>.
      </p>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>1.5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2.5</m></cell>
          <cell><m>3</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>f(x) = x^2</m></cell>
          <cell><m>1</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2.25</m></cell>
          <cell><m>4</m></cell>
          <cell><m>6.25</m></cell>
          <cell><m>9</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
      <p>
        These same five points are plotted below, along with the curve <m>y=x^2</m> itself,
        shown dashed just for reference &#8212; imagine it isn't there when you answer the
        next task.
      </p>
      <image width="55%">
        <shortdescription>
          The five points (1,1), (1.5,2.25), (2,4), (2.5,6.25), (3,9), plotted as dots, with
          the curve y = x squared drawn as a dashed reference curve through them, on blank
          axes from x=1 to x=3.
        </shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
            width=7cm, height=6.2cm,
            axis lines=middle,
            axis on top,
            xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
            xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
            xmin=0.5, xmax=3.6, ymin=-1, ymax=10.5,
            xtick={1,1.5,2,2.5,3}, ytick={1,2.25,4,6.25,9},
            clip=false,
          ]
            \addplot[dashed, gray, domain=1:3, samples=100] {x^2};
            \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2pt, blue] coordinates {(1,1) (1.5,2.25) (2,4) (2.5,6.25) (3,9)};
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-sketch-shape" workspace="0in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the graph above, using only horizontal and vertical segments, sketch a region
          using these five points whose <em>exact</em> area you could compute using
          rectangles to approximate the area under
          the curve from <m>x=1</m> to <m>x=3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-why-computable" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Why can you compute the exact area of the shape you just drew, even though you
          can't (yet) compute the exact area under the curve itself?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-left-right-riemann" workspace="0in">
    <title>Left and Right Riemann Sums</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Below are two more copies of the same five points and reference curve. On each one
        you'll build a staircase a specific way: on every subinterval between two
        consecutive points, draw a horizontal segment at one of the two heights available,
        then close it off with vertical segments down to the axis.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The five points (1,1), (1.5,2.25), (2,4), (2.5,6.25), (3,9), with the curve
            y = x squared shown dashed for reference, on blank axes for students to draw
            the left-rule staircase.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
              width=7cm, height=6.2cm,
              axis lines=middle,
              axis on top,
              xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
              xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
              xmin=0.5, xmax=3.6, ymin=-1, ymax=10.5,
              xtick={1,1.5,2,2.5,3}, ytick={1,2.25,4,6.25,9},
              clip=false,
            ]
              \addplot[dashed, gray, domain=1:3, samples=100] {x^2};
              \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2pt, blue] coordinates {(1,1) (1.5,2.25) (2,4) (2.5,6.25) (3,9)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            A second, identical copy of the same five points and dashed reference curve, on
            blank axes for students to draw the right-rule staircase.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
              width=7cm, height=6.2cm,
              axis lines=middle,
              axis on top,
              xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$},
              xlabel style={anchor=west}, ylabel style={anchor=south},
              xmin=0.5, xmax=3.6, ymin=-1, ymax=10.5,
              xtick={1,1.5,2,2.5,3}, ytick={1,2.25,4,6.25,9},
              clip=false,
            ]
              \addplot[dashed, gray, domain=1:3, samples=100] {x^2};
              \addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark size=2.2pt, blue] coordinates {(1,1) (1.5,2.25) (2,4) (2.5,6.25) (3,9)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-left-rule" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the LEFT graph: on each of the four subintervals, use the height of the point
          at its <em>left</em> end (so, for example, the piece from <m>x=1</m> to
          <m>x=1.5</m> gets height <m>f(1)=1</m>). Draw the resulting staircase, then add up
          the four rectangle areas to find its total area.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-right-rule" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          On the RIGHT graph: on each subinterval, use the height of the point at its
          <em>right</em> end instead (so the piece from <m>x=1</m> to <m>x=1.5</m> gets
          height <m>f(1.5)=2.25</m>). Draw the resulting staircase, then add up its four
          rectangle areas.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-over-under" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare your two totals. Which staircase leaves gaps under the curve (an
          under-estimate), and which one pokes out above the curve (an over-estimate)?
          Explain how you can tell from the picture, using the fact that <m>f(x)=x^2</m> is
          increasing on <m>1 \le x \le 3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <remark xml:id="rem-riemann-sum-name">
    <title>Naming What You Just Built</title>
    <p>
      Each of your two staircases is an example of a <em>Riemann sum</em>: an approximation
      to the area under a curve built by partitioning an interval into subintervals, choosing one
      height on each piece, and adding up the resulting rectangle areas. What you built are called the
      <em>left Riemann sum</em> (using left-edge heights) and the <em>right Riemann sum</em>
      (using right-edge heights), each with <m>n=4</m> subintervals.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-more-rectangles" workspace="0in">
    <title>More Rectangles, and a Limit</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Four rectangles was a choice, not a requirement. Open the Riemann sum visualization
        linked below, which plots <m>y=x^2</m> together with a slider for <m>n</m>, the
        number of subintervals, and reports the left- and right-Riemann sum totals as you
        change it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/80bb27b92b">Riemann Sum Visualization (Desmos)</url>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-check-n4" workspace=".75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Set the slider to <m>n=4</m> on the interval <m>[1,3]</m> and confirm the left- and
          right-sum values match what you computed by hand in Exercise 2.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-increase-n" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now increase <m>n</m>. Try <m>10</m>, then <m>50</m>, then as large as the
          tool allows. What happens to the left-sum value and the right-sum value as
          <m>n</m> grows?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-limit-idea" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Both sums appear to approach a single number as <m>n</m> gets larger and larger.
          That number is, by definition, the exact value of <m>\int_1^3 x^2\,dx</m>. Record
          the value you observe. Later in the course, the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus
          will let you find this number directly, without taking any limit by hand.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
